    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Summary Writing in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>Summary writing involves reducing a passage or text to a shorter version while retaining the main ideas.</p>
<p>Summary questions test your ability to <strong>extract relevant ideas from a passage</strong> in a way that shows you understand what you've read. They are closely related to comprehension questions.</p>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Essentials of Summary Writing</strong></h2>
<p><strong>1. Identification of Topic Sentences</strong></p>
<p>A <strong> topic sentence</strong> is usually the main idea of a paragraph. It often appears at the beginning or end of the paragraph and provides the central theme.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<p>In a paragraph about pollution, the topic sentence might be: <em>"Pollution is a major environmental problem affecting cities worldwide."</em></p>
<p><strong>2. Identification of Key Ideas</strong></p>
<p>Key ideas are the most important points in a passage. They support the topic sentence and provide details or explanations.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<p>In a passage about <strong>corruption</strong>, key ideas might include:</p>
<ul>
<li>"Corruption leads to poor governance."</li>
<li>"It discourages foreign investment."</li>
<li>"It increases poverty."</li>
</ul>
<p>In a passage about <strong>road accidents</strong>, key ideas might include:</p>
<ul>
<li>"Over-speeding is a major cause of accidents."</li>
<li>"Poor road conditions contribute to crashes."</li>
<li>"Drunk driving is a leading factor."</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Use of Context Clues</strong></p>
<p>Context clues are hints within the passage that help you identify specific points needed to answer questions. To use them effectively:</p>
<ul>
<li>Pick the keywords from the question</li>
<li>Register them in your memory</li>
<li>Look for them as you read through the passage</li>
</ul>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>The 4-Step Method</strong></h1>
<p><strong>Step 1: Read the Questions First</strong></p>
<p>Reading the questions before the passage helps you:</p>
<ul>
<li>Have an overview of what the passage is about</li>
<li>Know what to look for when reading</li>
<li>Read with a purpose</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 2: Read the Passage Carefully</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Read for understanding</li>
<li>Identify the key ideas needed for answering questions</li>
<li>Pay attention to important details</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 3: Return to the Questions</strong></p>
<p>After understanding the passage, go back and tackle each question individually.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Scan the Passage for Each Answer</strong></p>
<p>Use context clues to locate specific answers:</p>
<ol>
<li>Pick the keywords from the question</li>
<li>Hold them in memory</li>
<li>Scan the passage looking for those keywords or related ideas</li>
<li>Always confirm your answer with textual evidence</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Steps To Write A Good Summary</strong></h2>
<ol start="1">
<li><strong>Read the passage carefully</strong> to understand the main idea.</li>
<li><strong>Identify the topic sentence and key ideas.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Rewrite the main points</strong> in your own words using simple and clear sentences.</li>
<li><strong>Ensure the summary is shorter</strong> than the original passage.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid adding your own opinions, examples, or extra information.</strong></li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Worked Example</strong></h1>
<p><strong>1. Passage</strong></p>
<p>"Pollution is a major environmental problem affecting cities worldwide. It is caused by factors such as industrial waste, vehicle emissions, and improper waste disposal. Pollution harms human health, damages ecosystems, and contributes to climate change. Governments and individuals must work together to reduce pollution and protect the environment."</p>
<p><strong>Summary:</strong></p>
<p>Pollution is a serious global issue caused by industrial waste, vehicle emissions, and poor waste management. It harms health, ecosystems, and the climate. Everyone must collaborate to reduce pollution.</p>
<p><strong>2. Passage</strong></p>
<p>Having spent my professional life studying, observing, examining and operating on brains, I would argue that the human brain is the single greatest, most amazing, immeasurable, unfathomable wonder, not just of the world, but also of the entire universe.</p>
<p>Each human being has one of these marvels inside his skull. Not only have we been given such an undeniable gift, but we have also been granted ultimate authority and full responsibility for its use. How we use it is entirely up to us, we can use it the way we think.</p>
<p><em>Culled from "You Have a Brain" by Ben Carson.</em></p>
<p><strong>Summary Writing Questions</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>In two sentences, summarize why the writer considers the human brain a great wonder.</li>
<li>State two reasons the human brain is described as amazing in the passage.</li>
<li>In your own words, summarize the responsibility humans have over the brain.</li>
<li>What two main ideas are presented in the passage?</li>
<li>Summarize the passage in not more than 40 words.</li>
</ol>
<p>Answers</p>
<p>1. The writer considers the human brain a great wonder because it is amazing and beyond full understanding. It is the greatest wonder in the world and the universe.</p>
<p>2.</p>
<ul>
<li>It is the greatest wonder of the universe.</li>
<li>It is immeasurable and difficult to understand.</li>
</ul>
<p>3. Humans have full control over how they use their brains and are responsible for using them well.</p>
<p>4.</p>
<ul>
<li>The human brain is a wonderful creation.</li>
<li>Humans are responsible for how they use their brains.</li>
</ul>
<p>5. The human brain is the greatest wonder of the universe. Every person has one and is fully responsible for how it is used.</p>`
