    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Direct And Indirect Speech in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Direct Speech</strong></h1>
<p>Direct speech, also called quoted speech, is when we write or say the exact words spoken by a person. These words are enclosed in <strong> quotation marks (" ")</strong> and remain unchanged. It is often used in conversations, storytelling, and interviews.</p>
<p><strong>Examples of Direct Speech:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Mary said, <strong> "I am going to the market."</strong></li>
<li>Tunde asked, <strong> "Did you see my book?"</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Features of Direct Speech:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The exact words of the speaker are used and enclosed in <strong> quotation marks.</strong></li>
<li>The pronouns and verb tenses remain the same as in the original statement.</li>
<li>It is commonly used in <strong> dialogues, books, newspapers, and interviews.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Reported Speech (Indirect Speech)</strong></h2>
<p>Reported speech, also called <strong> indirect speech</strong>, is when we tell someone what another person has said, but we do not use their exact words. Instead, we change the sentence to fit the perspective of the reporting speaker.</p>
<p><strong>Examples of Reported Speech:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Mary said <strong> that she was going to the market.</strong></li>
<li>Tunde asked <strong> if I had seen his book.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Features of Reported Speech:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No quotation marks</strong> are used.</li>
<li>The pronouns and verb tenses may change to reflect the shift from the original speaker’s perspective to the reporter’s perspective.</li>
<li><strong>Reporting verbs</strong> like <strong> "said," "told," "asked," "explained"</strong> are used to introduce the reported speech.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Changes In Reported Speech</strong></h2>
<p><strong>1. Change of Pronouns</strong></p>
<p>Pronouns in reported speech <strong> change</strong> depending on the subject and object in the new sentence.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Direct:</strong> She said, <strong> "I love this dress."</strong></li>
<li><strong>Reported:</strong> She said <strong> that she loved that dress.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Change of Verb Tenses</strong></p>
<p>Verb tenses often change in reported speech, especially when reporting something in the past.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Direct:</strong> He said, <strong> "I am tired."</strong></li>
<li><strong>Reported:</strong> He said <strong> that he was tired.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Direct:</strong> She said, <strong> "I will travel next week."</strong></li>
<li><strong>Reported:</strong> She said <strong> that she would travel the following week.</strong></li>
</ul>
<table border="1" style="width:400px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Direct Speech</strong></th>
<th><strong>Reported Speech</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>am / is → was</td>
<td>"I <strong> am</strong> happy." → She said she <strong> was</strong> happy.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>are → were</td>
<td>"They <strong> are</strong> late." → He said they <strong> were</strong> late.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>will → would</td>
<td>"I <strong> will</strong> call you." → He said he <strong> would</strong> call me.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>have / has → had</td>
<td>"She <strong> has</strong> finished." → He said she <strong> had</strong> finished.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>can → could</td>
<td>"I <strong> can</strong> swim." → She said she <strong> could</strong> swim.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>3. Change of Time and Place Expressions</strong></p>
<p>When converting to reported speech, words that refer to <strong> time</strong> and <strong> place</strong> may also change.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Direct:</strong> She said, <strong> "I will go there tomorrow."</strong></li>
<li><strong>Reported:</strong> She said <strong> that she would go there the next day.</strong></li>
</ul>
<table border="1" style="width:500px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Direct Speech</strong></th>
<th><strong>Reported Speech</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>today → that day</td>
<td>"I met him <strong> today.</strong>" → She said she met him <strong> that day.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>yesterday → the previous day</td>
<td>"I was here <strong> yesterday.</strong>" → He said he was there <strong> the previous day.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>tomorrow → the next day</td>
<td>"I will visit you <strong> tomorrow.</strong>" → He said he would visit me <strong> the next day.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>now → then</td>
<td>"I am leaving <strong> now.</strong>" → She said she was leaving <strong> then.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>here → there</td>
<td>"I live <strong> here.</strong>" → She said she lived <strong> there.</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>4. Reporting Verbs</strong></p>
<p>In reported speech, <strong> reporting verbs</strong> introduce the statement. Some common reporting verbs include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Said:</strong> Used for general statements.

	<ul>
<li><strong>Example:</strong> She said that she was happy.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Told:</strong> Used when mentioning the person being spoken to.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Example:</strong> She told me that she was happy.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Asked:</strong> Used for questions.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Example:</strong> He asked if I had done my homework.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Explained:</strong> Used when giving detailed information.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Example:</strong> She explained that the exam would be next week.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<table border="1" style="width:400px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Usage</strong></th>
<th><strong>Direct Speech</strong></th>
<th><strong>Reported Speech</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>To quote someone exactly</strong></td>
<td>✅ Yes</td>
<td>❌ No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>To summarize what someone said</strong></td>
<td>❌ No</td>
<td>✅ Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>In books, dialogues, and interviews</strong></td>
<td>✅ Yes</td>
<td>❌ No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>When retelling events from the past</strong></td>
<td>❌ No</td>
<td>✅ Yes</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>`
