    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Concord in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>Concord, also called<strong> subject-verb agreement</strong>, ensures that the verb in a sentence matches the subject in<strong> number</strong> (singular/plural) and<strong> person</strong> (first, second, third).</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>She<strong> writes</strong> a letter</em>(singular subject + singular verb).</li>
<li><em>They<strong> write</strong> letters</em>(plural subject + plural verb).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Basic Rule of Concord:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>A singular subject takes a singular verb.</li>
<li>A plural subject takes a plural verb.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Concord</strong></h2>
<p>1. Grammatical Concord (Subject-Verb Agreement)</p>
<p>This is the basic agreement between the subject and the verb in a sentence.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The boy <strong> plays</strong> football. (Singular subject → singular verb)</li>
<li>The boys <strong> play</strong> football. (Plural subject → plural verb)</li>
<li>She <strong> writes</strong> a letter. (Singular subject → singular verb)</li>
<li>They <strong> write</strong> letters. (Plural subject → plural verb)</li>
</ul>
<p>In subject-verb concord, the subject (the person or thing performing the action) and the verb must agree in both <strong> number</strong> and <strong> person.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Singular Subject + Singular Verb:</strong>
<p>"She is going to the store." (Singular subject, singular verb)</p>
<p>"The teacher explains the lesson." (Singular subject, singular verb)</p>
</li>
<li><strong>Plural Subject + Plural Verb:</strong>
<p>"They are going to the store." (Plural subject, plural verb)</p>
<p>"The teachers explain the lesson." (Plural subject, plural verb)</p>
</li>
<li><strong>When subjects are joined by "and":</strong>
<p>"Mary and John are friends." (Two singular subjects, plural verb)</p>
</li>
<li><strong>When subjects are joined by "or" or "nor":</strong>
<p>"Either the dog or the cat is outside." (Singular subject, singular verb)</p>
<p>"Neither the teacher nor the students are here." (Plural verb)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>2. Notional Concord</p>
<p>This occurs when agreement is based on the meaning (or notion) rather than the grammatical form of the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The committee <strong> are</strong> divided in their opinions. (Though "committee" is singular in form, it refers to many people)</li>
<li>The team <strong> have</strong> won the match. (The focus is on individuals in the team)</li>
<li>The jury <strong> were</strong> unable to reach an agreement. (Emphasis on individual jury members)</li>
</ul>
<p>3. Proximity Concord</p>
<p>This happens when the verb agrees with the noun closest to it, especially with "either...or", "neither...nor", and "not only...but also".</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Either John or his brothers <strong> are</strong> coming.</li>
<li>Neither the teachers nor the principal <strong> is</strong> attending the meeting.</li>
<li>Not only the students but also the teacher <strong> has</strong> arrived.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>4. Pronoun-Antecedent Concord:</strong></p>
<p>Pronouns (like he, she, it, they) must agree with their antecedents (the words they refer to) in <strong> number</strong> and <strong> gender.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Number Agreement:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Singular Antecedent: "John is my friend. <strong> He</strong> is very kind."</li>
<li>Plural Antecedent: "The girls are playing, and <strong> they</strong> look happy."</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Gender Agreement:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Masculine Antecedent: "Alex is a doctor. <strong> He</strong> is skilled."</li>
<li>Feminine Antecedent: "Sarah is an artist. <strong> She</strong> is talented."</li>
<li>Gender-Neutral Antecedent: "The child is crying because <strong> they</strong> lost their toy."</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Indefinite Pronouns:</strong>
<p>Some indefinite pronouns are singular, like "everyone," "someone," and "nobody."</p>
<ul>
<li>"Everyone has his or her own opinion.".</li>
</ul>
<p>Some are plural, like "many," "few," and "both."</p>
<ul>
<li>"Many have tried to solve the problem.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Special Cases of Concord</strong></h2>
<p>1. Collective Nouns</p>
<p>Collective nouns (like family, team, committee) can take either singular or plural verbs depending on whether you're thinking of the group as a single unit or as individuals.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>My family <strong> is</strong> large. (considered as one unit)</li>
<li>My family <strong> are</strong> all football fans. (considered as individuals)</li>
<li>The team <strong> is</strong> playing well. (considered as one unit)</li>
<li>The team <strong> are</strong> wearing their new uniforms. (considered as individuals)</li>
</ul>
<p>2. Uncountable Nouns</p>
<p>Uncountable nouns always take singular verbs.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The information <strong> is</strong> useful.</li>
<li>The furniture <strong> needs</strong> to be moved.</li>
<li>The water <strong> looks</strong> clean.</li>
</ul>
<p>3. Compound Subjects</p>
<p>When two or more subjects are joined by "and", they usually take a plural verb.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Bola and Tunde <strong> are</strong> friends.</li>
<li>The book and the pen <strong> are</strong> on the table.</li>
</ul>
<p>However, when compound subjects refer to the same person or thing, they take a singular verb:</p>
<ul>
<li>Bread and butter <strong> is</strong> my favorite breakfast.</li>
<li>The writer and poet <strong> has</strong> arrived. (if it's the same person)</li>
</ul>
<p>4. Expressions of Quantity</p>
<p>With expressions of quantity (like "a lot of", "plenty of", "some of"), the verb agrees with the noun that follows:</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>A lot of people <strong> were</strong> at the party.</li>
<li>A lot of food <strong> was</strong> wasted.</li>
<li>Some of the money <strong> is</strong> missing.</li>
<li>Some of the students <strong> are</strong> absent.</li>
</ul>
<p>5. <strong> Agreement with "Each" and "Every":</strong></p>
<p>When <strong> "each"</strong> or <strong> "every"</strong> is used as the antecedent, the pronoun that follows must be <strong> singular</strong>, even though the noun may seem plural.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Correct Usage:</strong> "Each of the students has his or her own book."</li>
<li><strong>Incorrect:</strong> "Each of the students have their own book."</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>6. Agreement with "One Another":</strong></p>
<p>"One another" refers to a plural relationship, so plural pronouns should be used.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Correct Usage:</strong> "The players support one another."</li>
</ul>`
