    content: `<p>Literature refers to written works, especially those considered to have artistic or intellectual value. It is the art of written works that uses language to express ideas, emotions, and experiences in a creative and imaginative way.</p>
<h2><strong>Contents</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li>Prose.</li>
<li>Figures of speech.</li>
<li>Drama.</li>
<li>Poetry.</li>
<li>Character analysis.</li>
<li>Symbolism.</li>
<li>Literature Texts.</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Prose</strong></h1>
<p>Prose is written or spoken language in its ordinary form, without metrical structure or rhyme patterns that are found in poetry. It is the most common form of written language and is used in novels, short stories, essays, and most everyday writing.</p>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Prose</strong></h2>
<h2><strong>1. Fiction</strong></h2>
<p>Imaginative or invented stories that may be based on real events but are not strictly factual.</p>
<p><strong>Types of Fiction:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Novel:</strong> A long fictional narrative that typically features complex characters, plot, and setting.

	<ul>
<li>Example: "Things Fall Apart" by Chinua Achebe.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Novella:</strong> A fictional narrative shorter than a novel but longer than a short story.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "Animal Farm" by George Orwell.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Short Story:</strong> A brief fictional narrative that can typically be read in one sitting.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The New Tribe" by Buchi Emecheta.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2><strong>2. Non-Fiction</strong></h2>
<p>Writing that deals with real events, people, and information.</p>
<p><strong>Types of Non-Fiction:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Biography:</strong> An account of someone's life written by another person.

	<ul>
<li>Example: "Mandela: The Authorized Biography" by Anthony Sampson.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Autobiography:</strong> An account of a person's life written by that person.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "There Was a Country" by Chinua Achebe.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Essay:</strong> A short piece of writing on a particular subject.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The Trouble with Nigeria" by Chinua Achebe.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Memoir:</strong> A historical account or autobiography written from personal knowledge.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "Dreams from My Father" by Barack Obama.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Features of Prose</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Plot:</strong> The sequence of events in a story.

	<ul>
<li>Exposition (introduction).</li>
<li>Rising action.</li>
<li>Climax.</li>
<li>Falling action.</li>
<li>Resolution.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Character:</strong> People represented in the literary work.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Protagonist:</strong> Main character</li>
<li><strong>Antagonist:</strong> Character who opposes the protagonist</li>
<li><strong>Flat character:</strong> Simple character with one or two traits</li>
<li><strong>Round character:</strong> Complex character with many traits</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic character:</strong> Character who changes throughout the story</li>
<li><strong>Static character:</strong> Character who remains the same throughout the story</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Setting:</strong> The time and place in which the story takes place.
	<ul>
<li>Physical location.</li>
<li>Time period.</li>
<li>Social environment.</li>
<li>Weather/climate.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Point of View:</strong> The perspective from which the story is told.
	<ul>
<li><strong>First-person:</strong> Narrator is a character in the story (uses "I")</li>
<li><strong>Second-person:</strong> Narrator addresses the reader as "you"</li>
<li><strong>Third-person limited:</strong> Narrator knows the thoughts of one character</li>
<li><strong>Third-person omniscient:</strong> Narrator knows the thoughts of all characters</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Theme:</strong> The central idea or message of the story.</li>
<li><strong>Style:</strong> The author's choice of words, sentence structure, and literary devices.</li>
<li><strong>Tone:</strong> The author's attitude toward the subject or audience.</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Figures Of Speech</strong></h1>
<p>Figures of speech are words or phrases used in a non-literal sense to create a particular effect in speech or writing.</p>
<p><strong>Types of Figures of Speech</strong></p>
<h2>1. Simile</h2>
<p>A comparison between two unlike things using the words "like" or "as."</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Her smile is bright like the sun.</li>
<li>He runs as fast as a cheetah.</li>
<li>The marketplace was as busy as a beehive.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Metaphor</h2>
<p>A direct comparison between two unlike things without using "like" or "as."</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The classroom was a zoo during break time.</li>
<li>Time is money.</li>
<li>Her eyes were pools of wisdom.</li>
</ul>
<h2>3. Personification</h2>
<p>Giving human qualities to non-human objects or ideas.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The wind whispered through the trees.</li>
<li>The stars danced in the night sky.</li>
<li>Opportunity knocked at his door.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Hyperbole</h2>
<p>An extreme exaggeration used for emphasis or effect.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>I'm so hungry I could eat a horse.</li>
<li>She's been waiting for ages.</li>
<li>I have a million things to do today.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5. Onomatopoeia</h2>
<p>Words that imitate the sound they describe.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The buzzing bees flew around the hive.</li>
<li>The clock ticked loudly in the silent room.</li>
<li>Splash! The stone fell into the river.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6. Alliteration</h2>
<p>Repetition of the same consonant sound at the beginning of closely connected words.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>She sells seashells by the seashore.</li>
<li>The wicked witch watched the window.</li>
<li>Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7. Oxymoron</h2>
<p>A phrase that combines contradictory terms.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Deafening silence.</li>
<li>Living dead.</li>
<li>Original copy.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8. Irony</h2>
<p>The use of words to express something different from and often opposite to their literal meaning.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Saying "What lovely weather!" during a thunderstorm.</li>
<li>A traffic policeman's car getting clamped.</li>
<li>A fire station burning down.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9. Euphemism</h2>
<p>A mild or indirect word or expression substituted for one considered to be too harsh or blunt.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>"Passed away" instead of "died".</li>
<li>"Let go" instead of "fired".</li>
<li>"Senior citizen" instead of "old person".</li>
</ul>
<h2>10. Apostrophe</h2>
<p>Addressing an absent person, an abstract idea, or an inanimate object as if it were present and capable of understanding.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>"Death, be not proud" (John Donne).</li>
<li>"O Liberty, what crimes are committed in thy name!" (Madame Roland).</li>
<li>"Stars, hide your fires!" (Shakespeare's Macbeth).</li>
</ul>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Drama</strong></h1>
<p>Drama is a genre of literature represented through performance, dialogue, and action. It is meant to be performed on stage before an audience.</p>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Features of Drama</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Dialogue:</strong> The conversation between characters in the play.</li>
<li><strong>Stage Directions:</strong> Instructions written by the playwright about how the actors should move, speak, or express emotions.</li>
<li><strong>Acts and Scenes:</strong> Divisions within a play to organize the plot.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Act:</strong> A major division of a play</li>
<li><strong>Scene:</strong> A subdivision of an act, often indicating a change in time or place</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Script:</strong> The written text of a play, including dialogue and stage directions.</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Elements of Drama</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Plot:</strong> The sequence of events that make up the story of the play.

	<ul>
<li>Exposition.</li>
<li>Rising action.</li>
<li>Climax.</li>
<li>Falling action.</li>
<li>Resolution.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Characters:</strong> The people represented in the play.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Protagonist:</strong> Main character</li>
<li><strong>Antagonist:</strong> Character who opposes the protagonist</li>
<li><strong>Supporting characters:</strong> Secondary characters who help develop the story</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Setting:</strong> The time and place in which the drama unfolds.</li>
<li><strong>Conflict:</strong> The struggle between opposing forces in the play.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Character vs. Character:</strong> Conflict between characters</li>
<li><strong>Character vs. Self:</strong> Internal conflict within a character</li>
<li><strong>Character vs. Society:</strong> Conflict between a character and social norms</li>
<li><strong>Character vs. Nature:</strong> Conflict between a character and natural forces</li>
<li><strong>Character vs. Fate/Supernatural:</strong> Conflict between a character and destiny or supernatural forces</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Theme:</strong> The central idea or message of the play.</li>
<li><strong>Stagecraft:</strong> The technical aspects of theatrical production.
	<ul>
<li>Set design.</li>
<li>Lighting.</li>
<li>Sound effects.</li>
<li>Costumes.</li>
<li>Props.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Dramatic Irony:</strong> When the audience knows something that the characters do not know.</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center">Types of Drama</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tragedy:</strong> A serious play with an unhappy ending, typically involving the downfall of the main character.

	<ul>
<li>Example: "Romeo and Juliet" by William Shakespeare.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Comedy:</strong> A humorous play with a happy ending.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The Incorruptible Judge" by D.O. Fagunwa.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Tragicomedy:</strong> A play that combines elements of both tragedy and comedy.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The Government Inspector" by Nikolai Gogol.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Melodrama:</strong> A dramatic work characterized by exaggerated characters and exciting events intended to appeal to emotions.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The Lion and the Jewel" by Wole Soyinka.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Farce:</strong> A comic dramatic work using buffoonery and horseplay.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "Our Husband Has Gone Mad Again" by Ola Rotimi.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Theme and Setting in Drama</strong></h2>
<p>The theme is the central idea, message, or insight about life that the playwright wishes to communicate through the drama.</p>
<p><strong>Types of Themes</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Universal Themes:</strong> Ideas that resonate across cultures and time periods.

	<ul>
<li>Love and sacrifice.</li>
<li>Good versus evil.</li>
<li>Coming of age.</li>
<li>Power and corruption.</li>
<li>Justice and injustice.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Cultural Themes:</strong> Ideas specific to particular cultures or societies.
	<ul>
<li>Traditional values versus modernity.</li>
<li>Cultural identity.</li>
<li>Colonial experiences.</li>
<li>Tribal customs and beliefs.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>How to Identify Themes in Drama</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Analyze the title:</strong> Often provides a clue to the central theme.</li>
<li><strong>Examine conflicts:</strong> The central conflicts often point to the theme.</li>
<li><strong>Consider character development:</strong> How characters change often reveals themes.</li>
<li><strong>Note recurring images or motifs:</strong> Repeated elements usually emphasize themes.</li>
<li><strong>Study the resolution:</strong> How conflicts are resolved typically reinforces themes.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Setting in Drama</strong></p>
<p>The setting is the time, place, and social environment in which the drama takes place.</p>
<p><strong>Elements of Setting</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Time:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Historical period.</li>
<li>Time of day/year.</li>
<li>Duration of events.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Place:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Geographical location.</li>
<li>Physical environment.</li>
<li>Indoor/outdoor spaces.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Social Environment:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Cultural context.</li>
<li>Political climate.</li>
<li>Economic conditions.</li>
<li>Religious beliefs.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Functions of Setting in Drama</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Creates atmosphere:</strong> Establishes mood and tone.</li>
<li><strong>Influences characters:</strong> Shapes characters' values, beliefs, and behaviors.</li>
<li><strong>Provides context:</strong> Helps audience understand the social, historical, and cultural background.</li>
<li><strong>Creates conflict:</strong> Often serves as a source of external conflict.</li>
<li><strong>Supports theme:</strong> Reinforces and develops the drama's central ideas.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Types of Settings</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Realistic Setting:</strong> Detailed representation of an actual time and place.

	<ul>
<li>Example: A Nigerian village in the colonial era in "The Lion and the Jewel".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Symbolic Setting:</strong> A setting that represents abstract ideas or concepts.
	<ul>
<li>Example: The forest in "A Midsummer Night's Dream" representing freedom from social constraints.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Fantastic Setting:</strong> An imaginary or supernatural environment.
	<ul>
<li>Example: The spirit world in "The Gods Are Not to Blame".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Poetry</strong></h1>
<p>Poetry is a form of literature that uses the aesthetic and rhythmic qualities of language to evoke meanings in addition to, or instead of, the prosaic meaning.</p>
<h2>Features of Poetry</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Line and Stanza:</strong> The basic structural units of a poem.

	<ul>
<li><strong>Line:</strong> A single row of words in a poem</li>
<li><strong>Stanza:</strong> A group of lines arranged together, similar to a paragraph in prose</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Rhythm:</strong> The pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a poem.</li>
<li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> The repetition of similar sounds at the end of lines.
	<ul>
<li><strong>End rhyme:</strong> Rhymes that occur at the end of lines</li>
<li><strong>Internal rhyme:</strong> Rhymes within a single line</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Meter:</strong> The rhythmical pattern of a poem, determined by the number and type of stressed and unstressed syllables.</li>
<li><strong>Sound Devices:</strong> Techniques that enhance the sound quality of the poem.
	<ul>
<li><strong>Alliteration:</strong> Repetition of initial consonant sounds</li>
<li><strong>Assonance:</strong> Repetition of vowel sounds</li>
<li><strong>Consonance:</strong> Repetition of consonant sounds</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>Elements of Poetry</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Speaker:</strong> The voice that "speaks" in the poem (not necessarily the poet).</li>
<li><strong>Tone:</strong> The attitude of the speaker toward the subject.</li>
<li><strong>Mood:</strong> The feeling or atmosphere created by the poem.</li>
<li><strong>Imagery:</strong> Language that appeals to the senses.
	<ul>
<li>Visual imagery (sight).</li>
<li>Auditory imagery (sound).</li>
<li>Tactile imagery (touch).</li>
<li>Gustatory imagery (taste).</li>
<li>Olfactory imagery (smell).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Symbolism:</strong> The use of symbols to represent ideas or qualities.</li>
<li><strong>Theme:</strong> The central idea or message of the poem.</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center">Types of Poetry</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Sonnet:</strong> A 14-line poem with a specific rhyme scheme.

	<ul>
<li><strong>Shakespearean sonnet:</strong> Three quatrains and a couplet (ABAB CDCD EFEF GG)</li>
<li><strong>Petrarchan sonnet:</strong> An octave and a sestet (ABBAABBA CDECDE)</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Haiku:</strong> A traditional Japanese poem consisting of three lines with a 5-7-5 syllable pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Free Verse:</strong> Poetry that does not follow a fixed pattern of meter or rhyme.</li>
<li><strong>Ode:</strong> A lyric poem that praises or celebrates a person, event, or object.</li>
<li><strong>Ballad:</strong> A poem that tells a story, often set to music.</li>
<li><strong>Epic:</strong> A long narrative poem about the adventures of a hero.</li>
<li><strong>Elegy:</strong> A poem of mourning or reflection on the death of an individual.</li>
</ol>
<h2><strong>Imagery in Poetry</strong></h2>
<p>Imagery is the use of vivid and descriptive language that appeals to the senses (sight, sound, taste, touch, smell) to create mental pictures for the reader.</p>
<p>Types of Imagery</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Visual Imagery:</strong> Appeals to the sense of sight.

	<ul>
<li>Example: "The golden sun sank behind the purple mountains.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Auditory Imagery:</strong> Appeals to the sense of hearing.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The wind howled through the empty corridors.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Tactile Imagery:</strong> Appeals to the sense of touch.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The rough bark scratched against my skin.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Gustatory Imagery:</strong> Appeals to the sense of taste.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The sweet, tangy taste of orange lingered on my tongue.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Olfactory Imagery:</strong> Appeals to the sense of smell.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "The aroma of fresh bread filled the kitchen.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Kinesthetic Imagery:</strong> Suggests movement or tension in the muscles.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "His heart raced as he sprinted down the path.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Organic Imagery:</strong> Represents internal sensations like hunger, thirst, fatigue.
	<ul>
<li>Example: "Exhaustion seeped into his bones.".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Functions of Imagery in Poetry</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Creates vivid mental pictures:</strong> Helps readers visualize what the poet describes.</li>
<li><strong>Evokes emotions:</strong> Connects readers emotionally to the poem's subject.</li>
<li><strong>Establishes mood and atmosphere:</strong> Sets the emotional tone of the poem.</li>
<li><strong>Reinforces themes:</strong> Supports and develops the central ideas of the poem.</li>
<li><strong>Adds sensory dimensions:</strong> Makes the poetic experience more immersive and complete.</li>
</ol>
<p>Analyzing Imagery in Poetry</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify the type of imagery:</strong> Determine which sense(s) the image appeals to.</li>
<li><strong>Consider word choice:</strong> Examine specific words that create the image.</li>
<li><strong>Note patterns of imagery:</strong> Look for related or contrasting images throughout the poem.</li>
<li><strong>Connect to theme:</strong> Consider how the imagery supports the poem's central idea.</li>
<li><strong>Analyze emotional impact:</strong> Reflect on how the imagery makes you feel.</li>
</ol>
<p>Examples of Imagery in Poetry</p>
<p>From "Piano" by D.H. Lawrence:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Softly, in the dusk, a woman is singing to me; Taking me back down the vista of years, till I see A child sitting under the piano, in the boom of the tingling strings And pressing the small, poised feet of a mother who smiles as she sings."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This example contains:</p>
<ul>
<li>Visual imagery: "a child sitting under the piano".</li>
<li>Auditory imagery: "the boom of the tingling strings".</li>
<li>Tactile imagery: "pressing the small, poised feet".</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Character Analysis</strong></h2>
<p>Character analysis is the process of evaluating the traits, motivations, and development of characters in literature.</p>
<p>Aspects of Character Analysis</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Physical Appearance:</strong> How the character looks, dresses, and carries themselves.</li>
<li><strong>Personality Traits:</strong> The character's habits, mannerisms, and behaviors.</li>
<li><strong>Background and Context:</strong> The character's social, economic, cultural, and historical context.</li>
<li><strong>Motivation:</strong> What drives the character's actions and decisions.</li>
<li><strong>Relationships:</strong> How the character interacts with others and how these relationships influence them.</li>
<li><strong>Growth and Development:</strong> How the character changes throughout the story.</li>
<li><strong>Role in the Story:</strong> The function the character serves in advancing the plot or theme.</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center">Types of Characters</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Protagonist:</strong> The main character of the story.

	<ul>
<li>Example: Okonkwo in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Antagonist:</strong> The character who opposes the protagonist.
	<ul>
<li>Example: The District Commissioner in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Foil:</strong> A character who contrasts with another character to highlight particular qualities.
	<ul>
<li>Example: Obierika as a foil to Okonkwo in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Round Character:</strong> A complex, multi-dimensional character.
	<ul>
<li>Example: Okonkwo in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Flat Character:</strong> A simple, one-dimensional character.
	<ul>
<li>Example: Mr. Brown in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic Character:</strong> A character who changes significantly throughout the story.
	<ul>
<li>Example: Nwoye in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Static Character:</strong> A character who remains essentially the same throughout the story.
	<ul>
<li>Example: Ikemefuna in "Things Fall Apart".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>How to Analyze a Character</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Note character descriptions:</strong> Pay attention to how the author directly describes the character.</li>
<li><strong>Observe actions and decisions:</strong> Consider what the character's actions reveal about them.</li>
<li><strong>Analyze dialogue:</strong> Look at what the character says and how they say it.</li>
<li><strong>Consider relationships:</strong> Examine how the character interacts with others.</li>
<li><strong>Evaluate reactions:</strong> Note how the character responds to events and challenges.</li>
<li><strong>Track development:</strong> Observe how the character changes from beginning to end.</li>
<li><strong>Connect to theme:</strong> Consider how the character's journey relates to the work's themes.</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Symbolism in Literature</strong></h2>
<p>Symbolism is the use of symbols (objects, characters, figures, or colors) to represent abstract ideas or concepts.</p>
<p>Types of Symbols</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Conventional Symbols:</strong> Symbols with widely recognized meanings.

	<ul>
<li>Dove (peace).</li>
<li>Red rose (love).</li>
<li>Cross (Christianity).</li>
<li>Lion (courage, strength).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Literary Symbols:</strong> Symbols specific to a particular literary work.
	<ul>
<li>The green light in "The Great Gatsby" (hope, dreams).</li>
<li>The conch shell in "Lord of the Flies" (civilization, order).</li>
<li>The yam in "Things Fall Apart" (masculinity, prosperity).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Contextual Symbols:</strong> Objects or elements that gain symbolic meaning through context.
	<ul>
<li>Weather conditions (rain as cleansing or renewal).</li>
<li>Seasons (spring as rebirth, winter as death).</li>
<li>Colors (white as purity, black as death or evil).</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Functions of Symbolism in Literature</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Adds layers of meaning:</strong> Enriches the text with deeper significance.</li>
<li><strong>Communicates abstract ideas:</strong> Represents complex concepts in tangible forms.</li>
<li><strong>Creates unity:</strong> Connects different parts of the work through recurring symbols.</li>
<li><strong>Enhances themes:</strong> Reinforces and develops central ideas.</li>
<li><strong>Engages readers:</strong> Invites readers to interpret and find meaning.</li>
</ol>
<p>How to Identify and Analyze Symbols</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Look for repeated references:</strong> Objects or images that appear repeatedly.</li>
<li><strong>Consider titles and names:</strong> Often contain symbolic significance.</li>
<li><strong>Note items given special attention:</strong> Elements the author describes in detail.</li>
<li><strong>Examine context:</strong> Consider how the symbol relates to characters, plot, and theme.</li>
<li><strong>Research cultural significance:</strong> Some symbols have specific cultural meanings.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Examples of Symbolism in Literature</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>In Chinua Achebe's "Things Fall Apart":
	<ul>
<li>The yams symbolize masculinity and wealth.</li>
<li>The locusts symbolize the arrival of colonizers.</li>
<li>Okonkwo's compound symbolizes traditional Igbo culture.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>In Wole Soyinka's "The Lion and the Jewel":
	<ul>
<li>The village symbolizes tradition.</li>
<li>The school symbolizes modernity.</li>
<li>The dance symbolizes cultural expression and identity.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>`
